Q3. Can a top-level Java class be declared as private
or protected? Why or why not?

Interview Ready Answer

No.

A top-level Java class cannot be declared as private
or protected.

It can only be declared as:

• public
• default (package-private)

Reason

A top-level class represents a complete type that must
be accessible outside itself. If it were private or
protected, it would not be usable according to Java's
access rules.

Example

public class Student{

}

Valid

----------------------------------------

class Student{

}

Valid

----------------------------------------

private class Student{

}

Compilation Error

----------------------------------------

protected class Student{

}

Compilation Error

