======================================================================

Trap 1

Question

How many access modifiers are available in Java?

Correct Answer

Four

• private
• default (package-private)
• protected
• public

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 2

Question

Can a top-level class be private?

Correct Answer

No.

Only nested (inner) classes can be private.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 3

Question

Can a top-level class be protected?

Correct Answer

No.

Only nested classes can be protected.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 4

Question

Which access modifier provides the highest level of security?

Correct Answer

private

Because it is accessible only within the same class.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 5

Question

Is default access modifier the same as public?

Correct Answer

No.

Default (package-private) is accessible only within
the same package.

Public is accessible from anywhere.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 6

Question

Can a class in another package access a protected member
without inheritance?

Correct Answer

No.

Outside the package, protected members are accessible
only through inheritance.

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 7

Question

Can constructors have access modifiers?

Correct Answer

Yes.

Constructors can be:

• private
• default
• protected
• public

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 8

Question

Can methods and variables have all four access modifiers?

Correct Answer

Yes.

Methods and variables can use:

• private
• default
• protected
• public

---------------------------------------------------------------------

Trap 9

Question

Which access modifier is used most frequently for
instance variables?

Correct Answer

private

It supports Encapsulation by preventing direct access
to object data.

